2. MAP BOUNDARIES — CC BY 4.0
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

src/data/geo.json and src/data/hit.json are derived from the India state and
union territory boundaries published by the DataMeet India community:

    https://github.com/datameet/maps
    Licensed CC BY 4.0 — https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/

Required attribution, shown in the app on the map itself:

    "India state boundaries by DataMeet India community (CC BY 4.0)"

MODIFIED: simplified to about 2% of its original vertices (Visvalingam,
shape-preserving), reprojected conic conformal, and rendered to SVG path data.
See scripts/build-map.mjs and scripts/build-hitlayer.mjs.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. NEIGHBOURING LAND — NATURAL EARTH, PUBLIC DOMAIN
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

src/data/world.json is derived from Natural Earth's 1:50m Admin 0 (Countries)
dataset:

    https://www.naturalearthdata.com/
    Public domain. "No permission is needed to use Natural Earth. Crediting
    the authors is unnecessary."

Credited anyway, on the credits screen (#/credits) — this project credits
every third-party source it ships, required or not.

Added for Task 5: the map used to draw India alone, in unbroken pale blue on
every side, which is a picture that teaches a six-year-old India is an
island. This layer is the neighbouring land beyond India's own border —
Pakistan, China, Nepal, Bhutan, Bangladesh, Myanmar, Sri Lanka and a few more
within a generous box around India — drawn beneath India's own map, muted and
flat, never interactive.

MODIFIED: countries other than India, cropped to a box around India, ERASED
against India's own depicted boundary (not merely filtered by name — see
scripts/build-world.mjs for why an ISO filter alone is not enough), projected
with the identical conic conformal projection src/data/geo.json uses, and
simplified to SVG path data. See scripts/build-world.mjs.

